From 7c4a6902214ddedcb4a861002db0938149869677 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: StonkGuy <68372320+StonkGuy@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 10:54:18 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 7/9] Signals: run guest handlers with the Linux signal mask;
 optional signal trace

While a guest signal handler runs, Linux blocks (interrupted mask | sa_mask | the signal unless SA_NODEFER) and rt_sigreturn
puts the interrupted mask back. FEX blocked only (sa_mask | signal) at the host and never touched its own guest-visible mask
(CurrentSignalMask, what rt_sigprocmask reads) on handler entry or return. A handler that saves "the old mask", blocks a set
around a server call and restores what it saved, as Wine's server_enter/leave_uninterrupted_section does inside its SIGUSR1
(thread suspend) handler, therefore unblocked the signal it was running for and let it nest, which Wine's per-thread wait
stack and wake-up reply protocol are not written for. With Wine's fsync a thread blocked in futex_waitv can only be suspended
by that signal, and a Windows program that suspends and resumes threads (a garbage collector's world stop) wedges threads
blocked in socket/pipe reads: a reader stops waking although data arrives.

FEX_SIGNALMASKFIX=1 (SignalMaskFix) saves the interrupted guest mask in the context backup on handler entry, sets the guest
mask and the host mask to interrupted | sa_mask | signal (minus the signals FEX needs unblocked), restores the saved mask at
rt_sigreturn and re-raises signals that were recorded as pending and became unmasked.

FEX_SIGNALTRACE=1 (SignalTrace) logs host signal arrival, masking decisions, deferrals, delivery, rt_sigreturn, rt_sigprocmask
made inside a handler and kill/tkill/tgkill into a lock-free ring in /dev/shm/fex-<pid>-sigtrace (async-signal-safe), to follow
a signal from sender to handler.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
